  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft remains dominated by a strong \nhigh pressure system over the central CONUS. Despite this, a \nlower/mid- level low pressure system is present near the \nNebraska/Colorado border as a extensive line of storms have \nfired in eastern Colorado. Excluding the continued preset of \nheat, the storm potential is the forecast focus.\n\nThe storms have already produced a slew of wind gusts from\n60-70 mph in Colorado. The line of storms have breached the \nKansas border andone 50 mph wind gust has been recorded. It will\nneed to be monitored if the storms can strengthen the cold \npool. The same CAMs have the storms weakening as they enter \nKansas and beyond, so the severe window is expected to stay near\nthe border. However, if the storms overperform, gusts of up to \n60 mph are possible as far east as Garden City. Modeled \nsoundings have a gradient of dropping CAPE and climbing CIN with\nthe eastward extend. That said, PW values of 1.5\" and DCAPE of \n1500 J/KG will still be enough to produce a lingering severe \nthreat in the strongest storms. How quickly they die off will be\nclosely monitored. \n\nWednesday is forecast to be marginally cooler than Tuesday \nat around 3 degrees cooler CWA wide. Realistically, the drop \nwill not be noticed as it will still feel hot as the hot summer \nJuly stretch continues. A heat advisory will be issued for \nClark, Comanche, Pratt and Barber counties from 1 to 9 PM. \nTraditional heat precautions are advised including hydration and\ncooling breaks. CAMs have hinted at isolated thunderstorm \ndevelopment Wednesday evening, but the continuity has been low. \nEnsembles keep precipitation chances below 20% with the best of \nthese chances in the western zones. \n\nThursday is more of the same with very hot highs and marginal\nstorm chances. Highs are forecast mostly in the 100-105 degree \nrange with much of the southern counties at or above apparent \ntemperatures of 105 degrees. A Heat Advisory is expected to be \nwarranted for some of the counties Thursday. Long-ranged CAMs \nhave an MCS descending into central Kansas from Nebraska. \nDepending on the formation and path, it may clip the \nnortheastern counties in the forecast area with severe wind \ngusts. More time will be needed to monitor the forecasted \ndevelopment. Some later redevelopment may initiate convection in\nfar SW Kansas late Thursday, but severe impacts are not \nexpected at this time. \n\nA drier stretch is then expected to take hold into the weekend and \ninto next week. Temperatures will also be a tad cooler with \nhighs mostly in the upper 90s as opposed to the 100 degree days \nof this week. No weather headlines are forecast to be needed at \nthis time.",
